{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794502011,"nonce":"SVfcDwzhSDxLxuMjsENkjteN09A=","title":"Scrolling as rows: a scrolled frame should write a row, not repaint the visible content","message":"A scrolled frame is a full CPU paint of the visible content. The walker pushes the scroll offset as a transform around the children and the viewport clip before it (renderer/paint/motion.rs, \"Apply scroll offset (AFTER children inset clip so clip stays fixed)\"), so every wheel or momentum frame walks, paints and collects text for what is on screen. The runner forces a full paint for any scroll. Wanted: scrolling as rows, as bound nodes are, so a scrolled frame writes a row and the batch stays.\n\nDesign, from reading the code:\n- A scroll container whose content qualifies is a bound node whose pose is its ScrollPhysics offset instead of a MotionBindings value. Its children are painted once at the offset of that paint (the baseline) into the dynamic batch, carrying its ordinal, and a frame sets the row to the change since, in the table or resolved on the CPU as bound nodes are. Text, images and nested scroll containers ride on the same chain (four deep).\n- The viewport clip it pushes before the transform must stay fixed: record the node's ambient clip after its own children-inset clip, where a motion node records it before.\n- Culling: paint with the viewport widened by an overscan (about one viewport each way) so a scroll reveals painted content; when the offset leaves the painted window by more than half the overscan the row is declined and the next frame repaints around the new offset. Repaint cost is then once per overscan, not per frame.\n- Declined (as now, a full paint each scrolled frame): fixed or sticky children (counter-scroll transforms), glass, canvases, SVGs, 3D layers, a scrollbar until its thumb is a row of its own (thumb translate is the offset delta times the track to content ratio, the other way).\n- The runner: a scroll must stop forcing a full paint when the container is on rows, and a frame of rows while scrolling takes the fast path. Hit testing, hover and focus already read the physics offset, not the painted one.\n- Harness: a scroll scene (a list of labelled cards), scripted by Headless::scroll, held to the full paint as the motion scenes are, with frame times for each path.\n\nRelated: 122c606.","files":null}]}